<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Irsee</h1><p class="st-meta">1692 CE – 1802 CE · capital Irsee</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Benedictine abbey in the Allgäu that gained immediacy late and became known for its scholarship in science and mathematics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Irsee Abbey was founded around 1182 by the margraves of Ronsberg as a Benedictine monastery near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-kaufbeuren">Kaufbeuren</a>. Over the centuries the abbots expanded their rights, and in 1692 the abbey bought the jurisdiction and was recognised as an imperial abbey, immediate to the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot ruled a small territory of villages as a prelate of the Empire, with a seat on the Swabian bench of prelates. The monks rebuilt the abbey in baroque style and maintained a school and a respected natural sciences cabinet; Ulrich Weiss, a monk of Irsee, was a noted philosopher and mathematician of the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1802 and its lands given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>. The buildings later served as an asylum, whose patients were victims of Nazi euthanasia killings, and are now a conference centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-irsee.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Irsee" width="320" height="480"><figcaption>Image: Mattana · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbey_Irsee_101a.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1692 CE – 1802 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Irsee</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Irsee Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1747&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-irsee" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Irsee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4926 500 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Irsee lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5246 756L5229 694M5246 756L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1747&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-irsee">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
